MMRRII SSCCRRIIPPTTSS
       llllvvmm--aarr  understands  a  subset of the MRI scripting interface commonly
       supported by archivers following in the ar  tradition.  An  MRI  script
       contains  a sequence of commands to be executed by the archiver. The _-_M
       option allows for an MRI script to be passed  to  llllvvmm--aarr  through  the
       standard input stream.

       Note  that  llllvvmm--aarr  has  known  limitations  regarding  the use of MRI
       scripts:

       • Each script can only create one archive.

       • Existing archives can not be modified.

   MMRRII SSccrriipptt CCoommmmaannddss
       Each command begins with the command's name and must appear on its  own
       line.   Some  commands have arguments, which must be separated from the
       name by whitespace. An MRI script should begin with either a _C_R_E_A_T_E  or
       _C_R_E_A_T_E_T_H_I_N command and will typically end with a _S_A_V_E command. Any text
       after either '*' or ';' is treated as a comment.

       CCRREEAATTEE aarrcchhiivvee
              Begin creation of a regular archive  with  the  specified  name.
              Subsequent commands act upon this aarrcchhiivvee.

       CCRREEAATTEETTHHIINN aarrcchhiivvee
              Begin creation of a thin archive with the specified name. Subse‐
              quent commands act upon this aarrcchhiivvee.

       AADDDDLLIIBB aarrcchhiivvee
              Append the contents of aarrcchhiivvee to the current archive.

       AADDDDMMOODD <<ffiillee>>
              Append <<ffiillee>> to the current archive.

       DDEELLEETTEE <<ffiillee>>
              Delete the member of the current archive whose  file  name,  ex‐
              cluding directory components, matches <<ffiillee>>.

       SSAAVVEE   Write  the current archive to the path specified in the previous
              _C_R_E_A_T_E/_C_R_E_A_T_E_T_H_I_N command.

       EENNDD    Ends the MRI script (optional).

